A||N||4||DaveL||OH MY GOD ! Sudden Horrible Tick||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||05-07-2001||05:00 PM||||Drove my 87 SE 2.5 home last night, nothing out of the ordinary. It's always had a slight tick despite good oil maintenance, and Marvel Mystery Oil. Went out this morning and started it up to take off for work, and it was making this godawful noise, almost like a knock. Oil OK, guage shows good pressure. I'm going to take off the oil cap and listen to see if it's inside.<P>If this is a lifter sticking, or collapsed somehow, is this an expensive item to replace?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||05-07-2001||06:26 PM||||HEE HEE HEE...<BR>Hey Dave!<BR>Had the same thing happen on my 84...<BR>When the piston skirt fell off, the piston went sideways, and cracked the cylinder...<BR>Happy nightmares!<BR>crash... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||05-07-2001||06:43 PM||||Had that on my 85 (GT but this may apply)<P>It was a sticky lifter, did an oil change to 20w50 and it lubbed her up good, fixed.... but get some professional advice and avoid driving before you cause dammage||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||05-07-2001||08:11 PM||||My first impression is that if it was OK when you shut it off, and then it started immediately after starting it in the morning, I would look at lifters first. It's possible the lifter was collapsed by valve spring pressure overnight, and refuses to pump up again.<BR>The lifter is cheap, about 3 or 4 bucks usually, and they can even be rebuilt. The gasket set should run probably 30 or 40 bucks I guess. Isolating the messed up lifter can be accomplished by turning the motor over by hand and looking for the rocker that doesn't respond. If it's not obvious, you may have to use a dial indicator to figure out which one shows the least overall movement. <BR>BTW, Marvel Mystery oil is nothing more than recycled oil.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||05-07-2001||11:04 PM||||I think if I wanted to keep the motor, and if I went to the trouble of taking it apart enough to get to the lifters & getting a gasket kit, I'd replace <B>all</B> of the lifters.  And change oil + filter.<P>Having said that, I admit that when I shattered a lifter cap in my 1982 Cavalier (1.8-liter), I got the pieces out with a magnet and replaced just the shattered lifter.  But that engine wasn't a "keeper," to me, at least.  Used form-a-gasket, etc.     ||||reg||1
